  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 218 PM CDT Thu Sep 24 2026\n\nTonight, moisture from departing rain showers and light winds will \ncreate a prime environment for fog development. While confidence is \nhighest along the North Dakota/South Dakota border, nearly the \nentire forecast area is expected to see some sort of visibility \nreduction. Assuming winds stay calm enough, visibility may drop to a \nmile or lower at times. Fog that develops is generally expected to \ndissipate by mid-morning on Friday. \n\nFocus then shifts to the next round of rain set to move into the \nforecast area, which comes starting Friday afternoon and continues \ninto Saturday. A shortwave aloft will be the driving force behind \nthis rainfall, bringing rain into the forecast area from south to \nnorth. Mainly light showers are expected, although there may be an \nintermittent flash of lightning or two over northeastern South \nDakota and western Minnesota early Saturday morning. Highest \nrainfall totals will be along and east of the James River Valley, \nwhere up to a quarter of an inch can be expected, with perhaps up to \nhalf an inch falling under the strongest showers and weak \nthunderstorms. Areas to the west will likely only see a few \nhundredths at best. \n\nAfter a dry Sunday, the active pattern will continue early next week \ndue to an upper-level trough over the western CONUS developing a low \npressure center into the Northern Plains. This system is expected to \nbring a bit more rain to the area, and ensemble medians from Monday \nmorning through Tuesday evening currently sit at a widespread 0.75\"-\n1\" with the latest runs. Ensemble spread does give about half an \ninch of wiggle room on either side, so still a bit of uncertainty in \nthe total amounts. However there is a roughly 30-55% chance to see \nmore than an inch of rain through Tuesday evening, generally \nincreasing from west to east. Monday afternoon and evening is the \ntime frame for which both the heaviest rainfall and best chances for \nthunderstorms are. Some of these storms may be severe according to \nmid-range probability models. Current probabilities in those models \nwould be akin to a Marginal Risk, but no outlook is in place at this \ntime. \n\nAs the upper-level trough moves further east, a cooler airmass will \ntake hold, dropping high temperatures to the mid-60s, about 5-10 \ndegrees below normal for this time of year. Once the early week \nsystem moves out of the forecast area, ensembles are fairly \nconfident in a dry forecast for the rest of the week, finally \nbreaking the active pattern for a couple of days.",
